WEBVTT

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.900
-I got an interview. -Yorkshire?

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.500
-Mr. Harriet? -So damn late.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.340
-I'd almost given up on you. -I'm Farnham, Siegfried.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:10.660
-New assistant. -Oh, aye.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.820
-Another one. Mr. Farnham's new assistant. -Another one.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.580
-You are a handsome devil. -Tristan!

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:19.460
The only way we'll survive my brother is if we stick together.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.580
Siegfried's bark really is worse than his bite.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.380
Underneath it all, he's a good man.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.410
Stand up to him. He'll love you for it.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:01.830
- Welcome.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:06.330
- Morning, James.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.070
There you are.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:13.780
- Thanks.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:14.180
- Morning.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:03:00.000
that money in veterinary college it's all these trucks and motor cars now no one needs vets anymore

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:02.630
- Thanks, Mother.

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:03.640
- Ugh.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:04.640
- Something will come along.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:05.640
- What if it doesn't?

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:09.210
- I'll go, Dad.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:10.210
- No, son.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:12.470
You sit and eat your breakfast.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:13.470
- Your father's had a word.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.470
- I don't want to work on the docks.

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:18.470
- You should be thanking your lucky stars.

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:21.470
Men are queuing on the streets, begging for work.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:24.040
- I know it can't go on forever.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.230
- It's good to dream.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:30.000
But it's also good to wake up again and see the world as...

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.000
It truly is.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:33.140
Postman missed one.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:36.910
You know your father wanted to be a professional musician.

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:42.190
His bread and butter was the cinema, then the talkies come along and then it's all at the Swanee.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:46.660
I don't mean to be cruel. We just worry, love.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:50.710
I got an interview.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:51.710
You did?

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:52.710
You didn't.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:55.380
Daraby?

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:56.530
Yorkshire?

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:00.000
What if it's like the others?

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:01.460
No job at the end of it.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.420
They're not going to have me go all the way to Yorkshire for no reason.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.060
There was no mention of a wage in that letter either.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.160
He is better off on the docks, and that's all I'll say about it.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.220
Best get a move on if you're going to catch it.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:23.920
Just don't be disappointed if there's not a pot of gold at the end of the rainbow.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:26.310
Right on.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:27.360
It'd be good.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.000
Probably be home tomorrow anyway.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.140
Hold up.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:34.220
You're wonky.

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:38.580
You know I only took that job on the docks to see us through the lean times.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:40.690
You've got a dream.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:42.880
You've got to chase it.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.790
Thanks, Dad.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.580
Cheese and Pickle?

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.400
Here's your ticket.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:55.690
Now get on before you miss it.

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:59.640
Bye, Mother.

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:08.240
Good luck, son.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:25.380
It's in your eye.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:30.000
To God he gets it.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:11.760
It's a waste of bloody time.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.080
You won't learn out in there, you can't learn out there.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:36.320
Darabey.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:37.580
What was that?

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:39.440
Did he say Darabey?

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:40.730
Aye, Darabey.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.020
Jenny, set yourself down and don't push past.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.320
Sorry, a little terror always wants to go at the back.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:50.280
Oh, a cannon.

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:52.540
I think you dropped this.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:53.360
Thanks.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:55.470
I like Golden Bridge, it's the best.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:56.910
Thank you.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:58.110
On or off?

00:06:58.230 --> 00:06:59.050
I'm getting off.

00:06:59.530 --> 00:06:59.850
Thanks.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:44.450
Excuse me, this is Darabee, isn't it?

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:45.750
Darabee?

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:47.940
No, you want bus for Darabee?

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.840
The bus for Darabee.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:53.290
You don't have to talk, funny.

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:54.610
When's the next one?

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:56.850
I need to be in Darabee before four.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:58.710
No more buses till tonight.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:26.360
- Hello. - Mr. Harriet?

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:29.090
- Yes. Sorry I'm late. - I'd almost given up on you.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:36.540
I'm Mrs Hall. I keep house for Mr Farnham.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:42.350
Take care of my Saint Nick.

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:45.090
What happened to you? You're drenched.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:50.410
I missed the bus. I got off the bus.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:51.230
Hi.

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:54.880
She likes you. That's a good start.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:57.490
Jess is suspicious of most men.

00:09:57.490 --> 00:09:59.120
Rightly so, too.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.000
You can wait.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.760
in here. I'll make

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.460
some tea and fetch

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:10.010
Mr. Farman. Mrs. Hall, where's the

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:11.670
blasted thing? One moment.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:16.980
Pencil. Pencil, pencil, quickly

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:19.940
now we haven't got all day. Cute.

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:24.170
One down, God knows how many to go.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:30.000
I'm sorry, who are you? I see you've met

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.480
already. This is Mr. Ariot.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:35.480
Yes, of course. And why

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:36.460
are you here exactly?

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:39.130
You invited me, Mr. Farnham.

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:41.780
Did I? For the

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:45.580
assistant job. Excuse us a moment.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:52.470
We agreed there'd be no more.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:54.450
He's travelled all the way

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.510
from Glasgow. I don't give a damn how far he's come.

00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:59.190
He can just go back again. I'm telling that.

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:02.890
- Is he even qualified? - Nine months ago.

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:07.660
- What's his name? - James Herriot.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:14.870
- Give the boy a chance. - Mr. Herriot, I'm Farnon, Siegfried.

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:16.860
- Don't ask. - I'm James.

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:20.490
- Thank you for this opportunity. - Yes, well, let's see how we rub along first, shall we?

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:21.950
- Jacket? - You're wearing it.

00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:25.040
- Job list? - Pocket.

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:28.680
- Will you not stop for tea? - I don't care how he takes his darjeeling.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:30.000
I want to know he's up to the work.

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:31.930
Come along, Harriet. Don't just stand there.

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:33.260
Uh, are we not...

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:34.660
I thought this would be an interview.

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:37.260
I've got my references from the principal of Glasgow Veterinary College.

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:40.020
No, I'm not interested in a lot of flannel. Let's get cracking.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:41.580
'Til tonight, Mrs Hall.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:43.030
Good luck.

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:45.840
Grab that.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:47.900
Cat.

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:52.030
In the box. The box man.

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:54.860
Dispensary.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:56.530
Oh, a masculator.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:06.330
- That's the bodizo.

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:14.840
- Bring tears to your eyes.

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:16.280
- This is Mrs. Hall's domain.

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:18.010
Cooks a mean black pudding.

00:12:18.010 --> 00:12:19.090
Makes it herself, you know.

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:20.610
Place looks like an abattoir afterwards.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.310
This is the animal shed.

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:37.290
You'll take care of feeding them morning and night.

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:38.870
If successful, of course.

00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:44.650
Good.

00:12:44.650 --> 00:12:46.570
Turn the door.

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:48.490
Our practice covers most of the Northern Dales.

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:49.830
Swaledale, Wednesdaydale.

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:51.860
So you'll need your own car.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.110
Beauty, isn't she?

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:55.210
Stunning.

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:58.840
We keep this spare for the assistant.

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:00.000
Good little runner.

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:02.980
Aye, she looks...grand.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:22.980
Let's see what you're made of, shall we?

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:26.180
Let's jump forward.

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:40.300
The majority of our work comes from the small holdings.

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:44.530
We do get the occasional small animal, cats, dogs.

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:48.810
Look at that, short horned cow, you see?

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:50.300
- Gorgeous beast.

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:52.350
- Dying out now, of course.

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:54.350
Presians give so much more milk.

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:56.390
- As much as eight quarts per day.

00:14:56.390 --> 00:14:58.280
- And you think that's a good thing, do you?

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:00.000
- If a farmer can get more milk for the same amount

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:07.610
amount of effort. I suppose it is. You suppose? I mean... But at what cost? This place has a character

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:12.640
all of its own. The Shorthorns are part of that. With them gone, the Dales lose a little more of

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:25.560
what makes them so special. Where did it all start for you, wanting to be a vet? There was

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:30.000
a small city farm at the back of my school. Mm-hmm. It was like having parts of the

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:38.240
country in the city. I developed a real love for the animals there. Ah, well, you see, the animals are the easy part.

00:15:40.110 --> 00:16:00.000
The people cause all the bother. How do you, Mr. Farnham? How do you do, Mr. Sharp? This is Harriet, your assistant.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:06.970
Possibly oh, I know the one you had me worried. I thought the bank were coming for me. What seems to be the trouble

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:14.510
I've got a gelding gun running. Oh, well, we certainly don't want that. Is he foreign? I'm from Glasgow

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:25.050
Scotland that that one over there. Yes. Well, which foot is it? Well, I need to see him walk

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:30.000
I've dealt with many horses before yes lots. I bet you

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.730
- What about in real world? - This will be my first.

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:49.400
- Hold a coat for you? - From the way he's holding his head,

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:59.570
I'd say it was the offside four. - Good. - Right.

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:02.760
Let's have a look at this hoof.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:43.400
I'm sorry young man, I hope to have told you, it's a very friendly horse.

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:47.020
He always likes to shake hands.

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:51.640
No, no. I'd like to continue.

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:30.000
Easy boy, steady down boy, steady down, I see, you carry on young man, nice please

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:50.940
Looks like passing the foot.

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:56.370
Got it.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:03.130
Wait.

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:05.160
Now to disinfect the wound.

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:10.590
Touch of iodine.

00:19:10.590 --> 00:19:17.670
Dash of turpentine.

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:26.880
My god, Mr. Farnham.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:29.440
It's wonderful what science can do these days.

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:32.930
What shall we say to that, Tenbob?

00:19:32.930 --> 00:19:34.480
Aye, aye.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:56.300
Go back a different way.

00:19:56.300 --> 00:19:58.540
Over the Brankstone Pass and down Sealdale.

00:19:58.540 --> 00:19:59.570
I'd like you to see it.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:02.730
I've never used iodine in that way before.

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:04.040
Is it a new remedy?

00:20:04.040 --> 00:20:05.920
Oh, it has no practical purpose whatsoever.

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:07.860
But it never fails to impress.

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:10.470
Makes them feel they're getting their money's worth.

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:13.030
Time to earn your wings.

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:32.210
I didn't expect it to be so beautiful.

00:20:32.210 --> 00:20:35.290
One of the wildest spots in England.

00:20:35.290 --> 00:20:37.510
It's a fearsome place in winter, I tell you.

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:38.580
Marvellous.

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:41.080
You won't be saying that when the snow's eight foot deep.

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:43.820
I've known this path to be blocked for weeks on end.

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:46.410
I'll be sure to keep a shovel with me.

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:49.070
That's Hodden Hill ahead.

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:51.920
You need to keep your speed up or you won't make it up the other side.

00:21:00.860 --> 00:21:01.860
The brakes don't work.

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:03.860
That happens from time to time.

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:04.860
They're consistently inconsistent.

00:21:04.860 --> 00:21:06.860
How am I supposed to slow down?

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:07.860
Why would you want to?

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:10.720
We want to go faster, not slower, faster!

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:14.590
Have faith in yourself!

00:21:14.590 --> 00:21:15.610
Keep your foot on it!

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:50.900
- Oh dear, shall I get out and push?

00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:51.850
- Day.

00:21:51.850 --> 00:21:52.600
- It's not over yet.

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:18.580
He's lying on the ground, covered in muck, and I says to him, "He always likes to shake

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:19.580
hands."

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:23.860
"How do you, Mr Farned?"

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:24.860
"Evening, Nick."

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:26.300
"Two ales, please, Jen."

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:30.000
"You found your way all right then, lad?"

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:32.290
- Thank you.

00:22:32.290 --> 00:22:34.240
- Here, Farnon, Lady House wants to know,

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:36.010
what's to do with this cat of hers?

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:37.470
- I'll ask her to come into the surgery tomorrow

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:38.870
to pick him up.

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:40.890
- Oh, and I've got a sow I want to talk to you about.

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:43.440
- Yes, yes, Henry, just give me a moment to wet my whistle.

00:22:51.500 --> 00:22:53.860
- You're on very good health.

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:58.020
- Now, about this sow of mine.

00:22:58.020 --> 00:23:00.000
Seven piglets, all good enough.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:07.440
But run to litter. He's not getting a look in poor sauce. Oh now. That's a very common problem and one which will have to wait

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:11.390
If I have nothing to think I may beckon call I'll never stop

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:20.100
Hey Farnham, got effort going on three cylinders. Want seeing to?

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:26.950
Yes, Mr. Hanshaw. I'll be up first thing tomorrow morning. No matter then. I'll go see George Pandy

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:30.000
He's always happy to come out see his animals

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:37.250
that's certainly your prerogative he's a perfectly adequate substitute yeah hold

00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:42.590
on Dennis here what about my pigs um perhaps you could direct your questions

00:23:42.590 --> 00:23:49.910
to my new assistant your assistant you mean I've got the job how does four

00:23:49.910 --> 00:23:55.040
quid a week in full boards out well it sounds great I mean thank you

00:23:55.040 --> 00:24:00.000
trial period only mind you of course these gentlemen are looking

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:10.790
after you you all right finding your own way home good now let's have us another and then we'll get

00:24:10.790 --> 00:24:16.700
to this pig of mine they got your brew on tonight haven't they shout oh hi puts hairs on your chest

00:24:16.700 --> 00:24:22.110
if you're up to it not like that in that space i shouldn't really i want a clear head tomorrow

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:27.910
probably for the best it's a drink for yorkshire men isn't it oh hi not for city folk

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:31.420
Where's the Harmon one?

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:25.640
Mr. Harriet, what on earth are you doing?

00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:29.800
Feeding the cats. I got the job.

00:25:30.410 --> 00:25:37.070
did you have? Only two. Mr. Sharp was kind enough to let me sample his homebrew.

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:46.200
On your feet if Mr. Farnham wakes up and catches you in this state you'll be out on your ear. Now put them back.

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:08.260
No sign of Rip Van Winkle.

00:26:08.260 --> 00:26:12.710
I expect he's tired from a lot of travelling yesterday.

00:26:49.640 --> 00:26:50.890
- Morning, Mrs. Hull.

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:52.060
- Mr. Parnham.

00:26:52.060 --> 00:26:54.660
- Morning, James.

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:56.160
I hope you slept comfortably.

00:26:56.160 --> 00:26:56.950
- Very much so.

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:05.420
- Thank you.

00:27:05.420 --> 00:27:07.480
- Stay long at the Drovers last night?

00:27:07.480 --> 00:27:08.730
- Only for a couple.

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:10.500
Get to know the locals, you know.

00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:11.960
- And did you?

00:27:11.960 --> 00:27:12.800
- Did I what?

00:27:12.800 --> 00:27:14.270
- Get to know them.

00:27:14.270 --> 00:27:16.060
- Who?

00:27:16.060 --> 00:27:17.550
- The locals.

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:20.610
- The locals?

00:27:20.610 --> 00:27:25.530
- Oh yes, yes, I was, but I did, I think.

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:27.070
- Sorry, what was the question?

00:27:27.070 --> 00:27:28.970
- Oh boy's barely woken up.

00:27:28.970 --> 00:27:29.600
Coffee?

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:31.680
Oh, I don't usually actually...

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:34.030
Something the matter?

00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:38.140
No, no, it looks...

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:40.150
But there's just so much of it.

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:44.650
It's not the mountain we conquer, but ourselves.

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:48.360
What's this about Henry's pig?

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:49.660
Henry's...

00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:50.580
Mr. Dinsdale.

